&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Chile, Obstinate Memory&lt;/em&gt; (1997) and Post-Screening Conversation&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;A country without documentary cinema is like a family without a photo album.&amp;quot; &amp;mdash;Patricio Guzm&amp;aacute;n&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Due to the passing of Chilean exile director &lt;strong&gt;Patricio Guzm&amp;aacute;n&lt;/strong&gt; Tuesday, September 15, in Paris, Columbia University will be honoring the monumental legacy of one of the world&amp;rsquo;s foremost documentary filmmakers. For the occasion, we are pleased to be screening his landmark film &lt;em&gt;Chile, Obstinate Memory&lt;/em&gt; (1997) on a 35mm print.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The screening will be followed by an all women-panel of Chilean artists, filmmakers, and scholars who will talk about Guzm&amp;aacute;n&amp;rsquo;s legacy from a plurality of approaches and feminist perspectives. The conversation will be moderated by Associate Professor &lt;strong&gt;Elizabeth Ram&amp;iacute;rez-Soto&lt;/strong&gt; (Film and Media Studies, Columbia University School of the Arts), and guests include &lt;strong&gt;Cecilia Vicu&amp;ntilde;a &lt;/strong&gt;(artist), &lt;strong&gt;Daffne Vald&amp;eacute;s Vargas&lt;/strong&gt; (Colectivo Lastesis), &lt;strong&gt;Nicole Costa&lt;/strong&gt; (director of &lt;em&gt;The Journey of Mona Lisa&lt;/em&gt;), Associate Professor &lt;strong&gt;Mar&amp;iacute;a Jos&amp;eacute; Contreras&lt;/strong&gt; (Theatre, Columbia University School of the Arts), and more to be confirmed, so stay tuned!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is organized by the School of the Arts Film and Media Studies Program.&lt;/p&gt;
