&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Big American Writer: the Bilingual Jack Kerouac&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Jean-Christophe Cloutier, in conversation with Brent Hayes Edwards&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Jean-Christophe Cloutier talks with Brent Hayes Edwards about &lt;em&gt;Big American Writer: The Bilingual Jack Kerouac&lt;/em&gt;, his new study that recasts Jack Kerouac as an ethnic, bilingual author, demonstrating that his French-Canadian upbringing and background were formative to his breakthrough literary achievements. Though Kerouac has long been mythologized as an all-American figure, the &amp;ldquo;King of the Beats&amp;rdquo; was born Jean-Louis K&amp;eacute;rouac to immigrant parents from Qu&amp;eacute;bec and was raised in a French-speaking home in Lowell, Massachusetts. Throughout his career, Kerouac not only secretly composed French manuscripts but also served as a self-translator to negotiate between French and English. Following years of archival excavation, Cloutier demystifies the Beat writer&amp;rsquo;s complex compositional practices and techniques of self-translation to show that the author&amp;rsquo;s famed &amp;ldquo;Spontaneous Prose Method&amp;rdquo; was in part a reaction to his thwarted hope to live in French. The book further reveals that Kerouac pioneered new ways of capturing the orality of North American French in his manuscripts, inventing a scripturality that shares an indelible kinship with French Caribbean and other creolized traditions of the Global Francophone world. Offering a new lens on an often-misunderstood figure, &lt;em&gt;Big American Writer&lt;/em&gt; shows why Kerouac&amp;rsquo;s bilingualism changes how we understand his place in North American and world literature.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Jean-Christophe Cloutier&lt;/strong&gt; is Associate Professor of English and Comparative Literature at the University of Pennsylvania and a former Sheila Biddle Ford Fellow at Harvard University&amp;rsquo;s Hutchins Center for African and African American Research. He is the author of the award-winning &lt;em&gt;Shadow Archives: The Lifecycles of African American Literature&lt;/em&gt; (2019) and co-editor, with Brent Hayes Edwards, of&lt;em&gt; Claude McKay&amp;rsquo;s Amiable with Big Teeth&lt;/em&gt; (2017). His volume, &lt;em&gt;La vie est d&amp;rsquo;hommage&lt;/em&gt; (2016), gathers the original French writings of Jack Kerouac, and his edition of Kerouac&amp;rsquo;s longest French novel, &lt;em&gt;Sur le chemin&lt;/em&gt;, was released by Gallimard in 2023. An audiobook edition of Kerouac&amp;rsquo;s French writings, &lt;em&gt;Sur le chemin et autres &amp;eacute;crits en fran&amp;ccedil;ais&lt;/em&gt;, featuring readers from Qu&amp;eacute;bec to Haiti and passing through Acadia to Louisiana, is now available. He also translated two of Kerouac&amp;rsquo;s French manuscripts for the Library of America&amp;rsquo;s &lt;em&gt;The Unknown Kerouac&lt;/em&gt; (2016).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Brent Hayes Edwards&lt;/strong&gt; is the Peng Family Professor of English and Comparative Literature and the Associate Director of the Institute for Comparative Literature and Society. He is the editor of the journal &lt;em&gt;PMLA&lt;/em&gt;. His books include &lt;em&gt;The Practice of Diaspora: Literature, Translation, and the Rise of Black Internationalism&lt;/em&gt; (Harvard UP, 2003) and the English translation of Michel Leiris&amp;rsquo;s monumental 1934 &lt;em&gt;Phantom Africa&lt;/em&gt; (Seagull, 2017). His most recent publications are the co-edited volume &lt;em&gt;&amp;Eacute;crire le monde noir &lt;/em&gt;(Rot-Bo-Krik, 2024), a collection of the interwar writings of the pioneering Martinican intellectual Paulette Nardal, and &lt;em&gt;Easily Slip into Another World&lt;/em&gt; (Knopf, 2023), the co-written autobiography of the Pulitzer Prize-winning composer Henry Threadgill, which won the ASCAP Foundation Deems Taylor/Virgil Thomson Book Award in Pop Music, the Jazz Journalists Association Book of the Year Award, and the American Book Award. Edwards was a 2015 Guggenheim Fellow, and in 2020 he was elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Please join the Center for Political Economy&amp;#39;s &lt;a href="https://politicaleconomy.columbia.edu/political-economy-climate-idea-lab"&gt;Political Economy of Climate Lab&lt;/a&gt; on Wednesday, October 14 from 6:00-8:00 PM EST for a fireside chat, The Carbon Wave: A Story of Democracy, Parenthood, and the Race to Protect Our Planet with &lt;a href="https://www.leahstokes.com/" target="_blank"&gt;Leah Stokes&lt;/a&gt;, Anton Vonk Associate Professor of Environmental Politics at UC Santa Barbara.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dr. Stokes will be in conversation with &lt;a href="https://polisci.columbia.edu/content/nikhar-gaikwad" target="_blank"&gt;Nikhar Gaikwad&lt;/a&gt;, Assistant Professor, Department of Political Science Columbia University to discuss Stokes&amp;rsquo;s book the &lt;em&gt;The Carbon Wave: A Story of Democracy, Parenthood, and the Race to Protect Our Planet&lt;/em&gt;, which tells the story of the largest federal climate law in American history, The Inflation Reduction Act of 2022. The book&amp;rsquo;s plot is told through the perspectives of three new parents working in and out of government to craft the package in a precious window of political opportunity. Author Leah Stokes traces the victories and setbacks that marked the bill&amp;rsquo;s trajectory, chronicling moments of tense negotiation between President Joe Biden, Senator Chuck Schumer, and Senator Joe Manchin. Stokes&amp;rsquo; storytelling combines memoir-style narrative of her own high-risk pregnancy with in-depth retellings of the talks that shaped the final law.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.leahstokes.com/" target="_blank"&gt;Dr. Leah Stokes&lt;/a&gt;, University of California, Santa Barbara, has been recognized by Time Magazine 100, who noted that she was &amp;ldquo;a power&amp;shy;house contributor&amp;rdquo; to the 2022 Inflation Reduction Act, which made the single largest investment in climate and energy in American history.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

