&lt;p&gt;Please join the East Central European Center, the Harriman Institute, and the Institute for Israel and Jewish Studies for the first installment of the Polish-Jewish Past and Present series, featuring a book talk by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Sarah Zarrow&lt;/strong&gt;. Professor Emerita&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Barbara Kirschenblatt-Gimblett&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;(NYU) and Professor&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/rebecca-kobrin/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rebecca Kobrin&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;(Columbia) will serve as discussants. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/malgorzata-mazurek/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Małgorzata Mazurek&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Displays of Belonging&lt;/em&gt; illuminates the lives and work of Polish Jewish collectors and museologists, who sought to preserve the treasures of the Jewish past while demonstrating Jewish belonging on Polish soil. As Jews comfortable in the Polish language and within Polish artistic and academic society, they saw themselves as intermediaries between less-integrated Jews and the Polish cultural elite.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;By tracing emergent documentation and display practices in partitioned Poland and in the interwar Second Polish Republic, Sarah Ellen Zarrow offers a better understanding of how integrated Jews identified with Polish culture and history and with non-Jewish Poles, and how they conceived of, negotiated, and argued their collective place within Poland. &lt;em&gt;Displays of Belonging&lt;/em&gt; offers a nuanced understanding of the many ways Jews in Poland saw their present and dreamed of their future. It places Jewish ethnographic practice and art collection within a Polish context and sheds light on how ideas about belonging and national identity were negotiated in museum spaces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gary Wolf and Nick Thompson discuss what our devices get right and wrong about us&amp;mdash;and how journalists can separate meaningful insight from technological hype.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Tow Center for Digital Journalism is hosting a conversation between two leading voices in technology journalism: Gary Wolf, cofounder of the Quantified Self movement and a longtime&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;WIRED&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;writer and editor, and Nicholas Thompson, CEO of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Atlantic&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;and former editor-in-chief of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;WIRED&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;From fitness trackers and sleep scores to health apps and AI-generated medical advice, technology increasingly guides highly personal decisions in daily life. But how much should we trust what our devices say?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Drawing on Wolf&amp;rsquo;s new book,&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Quantified Self: Learning to Observe&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, and Thompson&amp;rsquo;s&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Running Ground&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, which explores running, ambition, aging, and the limits of the body, they will examine what personal data can&amp;mdash;and cannot&amp;mdash;reveal about our lives. Bringing together Wolf&amp;rsquo;s pioneering work on self-tracking and Thompson&amp;rsquo;s perspective as a media leader, technology journalist, author, and host of &amp;ldquo;The Most Interesting Thing in AI,&amp;rdquo; the conversation will explore how journalists and the public can evaluate the claims made by wearable companies, health platforms, and AI systems&amp;mdash;and distinguish meaningful insight from hype.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Wolf and Thompson will also consider how technology journalism has evolved over the past two decades and what responsible reporting looks like when technologies increasingly shape how we understand ourselves.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The program will be followed by an audience Q&amp;amp;A and book signing. Copies of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Quantified Self: Learning to Observe&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Running Ground&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;will be available for purchase from The Literary Assembly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/the-quantified-self-what-can-our-data-really-tell-us-tickets-2000272428680?aff=oddtdtcreator" target="_blank"&gt;RSVP required&lt;/a&gt;. Registration closes 24 hours before event start time.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

