&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.ircpl.columbia.edu/calendar/interfaith-dialogue-in-theory-and-in-practice" target="_blank"&gt;Interfaith Dialogue in Theory and Practice&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Date: &lt;/strong&gt;Thursday, October 15, 2026 from 12:30-2:00PM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Location:&lt;/strong&gt; Casa Hisp&amp;aacute;nica&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Series:&lt;/strong&gt; American Body Politics&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speakers:&lt;/strong&gt; Mark Fowler (Tanenbaum Center for Interreligious Understanding) and Heather Miller Rubens (Institute for Islamic, Christian, and Jewish Studies)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Moderator&lt;/strong&gt;: Seth Kimmel (Columbia University)&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At moments of political and social crisis, conversation offers the possibility of compromise, if not also reconciliation and understanding. When religious histories and tensions run through these crises&amp;mdash;as they so often do&amp;mdash;many of us might think of turning to that particular subset of conversation known as &amp;ldquo;interfaith dialogue.&amp;rdquo; How do advocates and practitioners of interfaith dialogue understand and promote its protocols? How do they chart its origins and aims? How do they imagine the local scale of interfaith dialogue, which necessarily takes place among a specific subset of individuals located in a particular place and time, and its larger geopolitical and theological stakes? How does interfaith dialogue relate to pluralism and multiculturalism? Following concise presentations, our speakers will reflect on these questions while entering into conversation with each other and with the audience.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Free and open to the public &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSc-fDxiynaWA0T6KOQccY0O2BvxwLqG5e7Y7xE-H_095i0rTw/viewform" target="_blank"&gt;Please register&lt;/a&gt; in advance to reserve your spot&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All prospective attendees must register by 11:59PM on Wednesday, October 14, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pier Mattia Tommasino&lt;/strong&gt; (Columbia University)&lt;strong&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;in conversation with &lt;strong&gt;Davide Trentacoste&lt;/strong&gt; (Scuola Normale Superiore), on the occasion of the publication of Davide Trentacoste&amp;rsquo;s book:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Shahs and Grand Dukes. Medici-Safavid Diplomatic Relations in the Early Modern Mediterranean&lt;/em&gt;. Abingdon, Oxon. New York, NY: Routledge, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The early modern period witnessed the emergence of new forms of diplomatic interaction across political, religious, and cultural boundaries. Within this changing diplomatic landscape, Safavid Persia became an important interlocutor for European powers seeking to navigate the geopolitical balance of the Mediterranean and, in particular, their relations with the Ottoman Empire. This book examines the long-standing but still understudied relationship between the Medici Grand Duchy of Tuscany and the Safavid dynasty, from the late sixteenth century to the fall of the Safavids in the eighteenth century.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This event is part of the Italian and Mediterranean Colloquium organized by the Department of Italian and sponsored by the European Institute.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

