&lt;p&gt;Join the Weinberg Family Cerebral Palsy Center&amp;nbsp;for the October session of&amp;nbsp;the&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Movement&amp;nbsp;Recovery&amp;nbsp;Seminar&amp;nbsp;Series, &lt;/strong&gt;a&amp;nbsp;monthly seminar series for the community of scientist and clinicians working towards the common goal of recovery of function.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker: Kimberly Waddell, PhD, MSCI, OTR/L&lt;/strong&gt;, Assistant Professor, &amp;nbsp;Penn Physical Medicine &amp;amp; Rehabilitation,&amp;nbsp;Research Scientist | VA Center for Health Equity Research and Promotion&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Topic:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;From Neurorehabilitation to Health Promotion: Leveraging Behavioral Economics for Long-Term Health&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This talk will explore how behavioral economic principles, gamification, social incentives, and remote monitoring technologies can be leveraged to promote physical activity and long-term health among adults living with neurologicaldisease. Drawing on research in adults with stroke and Parkinson&amp;rsquo;s disease, Dr. Waddell will highlight innovative approaches to translating behavioral science and digital health technologies into interventions that support sustained physical activity beyond&amp;nbsp;traditional rehabilitation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dr. Waddell&amp;#39;s&amp;nbsp;research focuses on promoting long-term health and improving disease self-management and physical activity among adults with neurologic disease. She uses behavioral&amp;nbsp;science, including behavioral economic principles, to develop and evaluate interventions designed to support sustained health behaviors after neurologic disease. She also leverages administrative and real-world data to better understand post-stroke recovery&amp;nbsp;and disability and to identify patient populations who may benefit from remote monitoring and other approaches to improve long-term health.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;CTLgrads Learning Community - &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Scaffolding Historical Consciousness in the Classroom &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;(Session 2) - for graduate students&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Developing students&amp;rsquo; historical consciousness is a pedagogically valuable goal and applicable across all disciplines. This Learning Community explores the affordances of historical thinking as a pedagogical resource, as well as the challenges of incorporating historical thinking into teaching and learning. Coming from Architectural History and Music Theory, Kian and Jon bring firsthand experience thinking historically in their own fields, and are motivated to explore it further. Attendees will consider thinking historically as a pedagogical resource, discuss different strategies for incorporating historical thinking in course design, and workshop techniques towards utilizing history in the classroom as a learning tool..&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This second session of two-part Learning Community will be held in-person&lt;/strong&gt; and co-facilitated by Jon Crompton and Kian Hosseinnia, Senior Lead Teaching Fellows at the Center for Teaching and Learning Community. Participants should register for and attend &lt;strong&gt;both&lt;/strong&gt; sessions of this LC.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In Session 2, &lt;/strong&gt;attendees will work with particular objects and ideas that appear in their own pedagogical contexts, in two categories.First, at the level of course design: each participant will bring a syllabus or lesson plan that they have taught (or would like to teach) and strategize ways to increase the historical dimension of the course in connection with a course/lesson learning goal. Second, we will tackle ways that historical thinking can be brought into the classroom as a pedagogical tool. Participants will work with a particular object of their choice that comes from their discipline (a painting, a formula, a diagram, a technical term) and propose ways to introduce the object historically in the classroom through techniques of historical infusion: e.g., etymology, &amp;ldquo;vertical&amp;rdquo; vs. &amp;ldquo;horizontal&amp;rdquo; historical contextualization).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Session 1 will convene Friday 10/09, 1:10-2:25pm in room 212 Butler Library; registration is available &lt;a href="https://events.columbia.edu/cal/event/eventView.do?calPath=%2Fpublic%2Fcals%2FMainCal&amp;amp;guid=CAL-00bbdb7c-a0a4219b-01a0-a54cf4f3-00002439events@columbia.edu&amp;amp;recurrenceId=" target="_blank"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Session 2 will convene Friday 10/16, 1:10-2:25pm in room 212 Butler Library.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each session of this LC counts as a Pedagogy Workshop for the &lt;a href="https://tdp.ctl.columbia.edu/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Teaching Development Program (TDP)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. See the &lt;a href="https://tdp.ctl.columbia.edu/about/faqs/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TDP FAQs&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; for more information about obtaining credit for this and other graduate student-led events through the CTL.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;I Will Be There After My Wandering&amp;quot; (Documentary Film, 2025, Dir. Zhang Yang): Revisiting the Transpacific of Wan-go Weng (1918-2020)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Join us for the American premiere of the documentary &lt;em&gt;I Will Be There After My Wandering&lt;/em&gt; (2025), directed by Zhang Yang and produced by Suzhou Broadcasting System. The film explores Chinese American filmmaker Wan-go Weng&amp;#39;s long, prolific and under-recognized filmmaking career, and is based on Weng&amp;#39;s original 16mm film reels, generously&amp;nbsp;gifted by Weng, and carefully&amp;nbsp;preserved and managed by the staff of the C.V. Starr East Asian Library and colleagues at Columbia University Libraries.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A panel discussion with director Zhang Yang, faculty members, and librarians will highlight&amp;nbsp;the vital collaboration between faculty and libraries in using unique primary resources for teaching and research&amp;mdash;and in keeping this part of Chinese American history alive. &lt;em&gt;Reception following&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

